# $CLAUDE_DIR/stack.env — API keys and models for the multi-agent stack (chmod 600; $CLAUDE_DIR is
# ~/.claude unless CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR overrides it).
# The MCP servers read this file themselves (no reinstall after editing it), and each one gets only
# its own keys. Your shell profile (bin/with-stack-env --print-env) exports just the keys CLI tools
# read — STACK_EXPORT at the end of this file — and only when they are NON-EMPTY, so an empty line
# here never blanks a token you export elsewhere, and the image, Exa, Jina and Spider keys stay out
# of your shells, the agents' Bash calls and third-party MCP processes. Leave a value empty to skip
# that service. Values follow shell syntax: KEY=value, KEY="value", an optional trailing
# "# comment"; $HOME is expanded only by the stack's own Python MCP servers (image-studio, libdocs).
# Upgrades append new variables from stack.env.example here, commented out (the image and Claude
# models set to their defaults), and never change a value you wrote.
#
# Remote MCP servers (exa, jina, huggingface, wandb) get their keys at connection time from
# $CLAUDE_DIR/bin/mcp-headers, which reads this file: no key is written to ~/.claude.json or
# passed on a command line, and a changed key takes effect at the next connection (new session
# or /mcp → Reconnect). Re-run ./install.sh only after adding WANDB_API_KEY for the first time
# (the wandb server is registered only when a key exists). --mcp-plan previews MCP changes.

# Claude models. Agents name a family alias (model: opus / sonnet) and Claude Code resolves it through
# ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_<FAMILY>_MODEL; these lines are the stack's single source of the specific IDs.
# install.sh copies the non-empty ones into the env block of $CLAUDE_DIR/settings.json, which Claude
# Code applies to the terminal CLI, `claude -p`, the Agent SDK and the Desktop Code tab: re-run
# ./install.sh after a change (empty or commented out = Claude Code's own alias target). After an Opus
# or Sonnet change, /stack-doctor warns until the soft token limits and maxTurns are re-derived
# (tests/derive_thresholds.py; the record is MEASURED_MODELS in bin/doctor.sh).
# IDs from https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview (checked 2026-10-02).
ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# The haiku alias and Claude Code's background work (titles, summaries). The stack runs no Haiku:
# this slot runs Sonnet. Today's Haiku is claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 (same source, same date).
ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5

# Images (designer, image-director) come from image-studio: generate_svg and edit_image through
# OpenRouter, generate_image through Opper; a missing key disables only the tools that need it.
# Opper — generate_image: photographs and other raster images. https://platform.opper.ai
OPPER_API_KEY=
# OpenRouter — generate_svg (vector art) and edit_image (edits, retouching, composites).
# https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys — an account limited to some providers must allow the ones your
# models run on: recraft and sourceful for the defaults (https://openrouter.ai/settings/preferences).
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# The model behind each image tool: any image model of that tool's provider (empty = the default
# written here). image-studio looks it up in the provider's catalog before a paid call, and
# /stack-doctor shows the models in use. A change applies from the next image-director or designer run.
# generate_svg — an OpenRouter model with SVG output. Default: Recraft V4.1 Pro Vector ($0.30 an
# image). Models: https://openrouter.ai/models?output_modalities=image
IMAGE_STUDIO_SVG_MODEL=recraft/recraft-v4.1-pro-vector
# generate_image — an Opper image model. Default: GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst ($0.006-$0.21 an image, by
# quality). Models: https://api.opper.ai/v3/images/models
IMAGE_STUDIO_IMAGE_MODEL=openai/gpt-image-2.5-sunburst
# edit_image — an OpenRouter model that takes input images. Default: Riverflow V2.5 Pro ($0.13-$0.17
# an image, by resolution).
IMAGE_STUDIO_EDIT_MODEL=sourceful/riverflow-v2.5-pro
# Where generated images go when an agent names no folder (optional; an older
# OPENROUTER_IMAGE_OUT_DIR line still works)
IMAGE_STUDIO_OUT_DIR=$HOME/Pictures/image-studio

# Exa — semantic web search / fetch (mcp__exa). https://dashboard.exa.ai/api-keys  (empty = keyless,
# rate-limited; a key raises the limits)
EXA_API_KEY=
# Jina — reader, arXiv/SSRN search, PDF extraction, screenshots, rerank (mcp__jina).
# https://jina.ai/api-dashboard  — effectively required: without a key read_url, search_arxiv,
# extract_pdf and capture_screenshot_url refuse (agents then fall back to WebFetch).
JINA_API_KEY=
# Spider — crawling/scraping at scale (researcher). https://spider.cloud/api-keys
SPIDER_API_KEY=

# Per-call caps for exa, jina and spider, and Spider's anti-bot defaults or polite mode, applied by
# hooks/web_caps.py (PreToolUse; read at each call, no reinstall). Commented out = the default shown,
# which lives in the hook's KNOBS table; 0 turns a numeric cap off. Values an agent passes within a cap
# are kept; omitted options are filled. Spider paid options (premium/residential proxies x1.2-2, the
# unblocker +10-40 credits a page, browser sessions) bill your Spider plan; agents buy nothing.
# Exa: results per search, subpages per result, characters per page, characters per call, live-crawl ms
#EXA_MAX_RESULTS=15
#EXA_MAX_SUBPAGES=3
#EXA_MAX_PAGE_CHARS=10000
#EXA_MAX_TOTAL_CHARS=80000
#EXA_MAX_LIVECRAWL_MS=15000
# Jina: results per query (server default 30), read_url passages and words per passage, rerank top_n
#JINA_MAX_RESULTS=10
#JINA_MAX_TOPK=10
#JINA_MAX_CHUNK_WORDS=1000
#JINA_MAX_RERANK=20
# Spider caps: crawl pages when omitted (Spider's default is every page), pages per crawl call, depth
# when omitted and at most, search results, links, crawl delay ms, output format when omitted
#SPIDER_DEFAULT_PAGES=25
#SPIDER_MAX_PAGES=100
#SPIDER_DEFAULT_DEPTH=3
#SPIDER_MAX_DEPTH=10
#SPIDER_MAX_RESULTS=10
#SPIDER_MAX_LINKS=500
#SPIDER_MAX_DELAY_MS=10000
#SPIDER_RETURN_FORMAT=markdown
# Spider request mode (smart, chrome, http)
#SPIDER_REQUEST=smart
# Spider mode. 1: anti-bot defaults (below). 0: polite mode — fills respect_robots=true, a crawl delay in
# ms filled and enforced as a floor (Spider then fetches one page at a time) and a concurrency_limit cap,
# sends fingerprint=false, and refuses respect_robots=false, spider_unblocker, spider_browser_open,
# spider_ai_browser (stealth escalation, CAPTCHA solving), proxy_enabled/proxy/remote_proxy/country_code,
# fingerprint=true, user_agent and cookies
#SPIDER_ANTIBOT=1
#SPIDER_DELAY_MS=1000
#SPIDER_CONCURRENCY=2
# Spider anti-bot (SPIDER_ANTIBOT=1): browser fingerprint, premium proxies on every call
# (spider_unblocker always uses them), proxy pool (residential, mobile, isp, datacenter), proxy
# country (ISO code, empty = Spider's choice); either mode: seconds to wait for an idle network on
# unblocker and chrome calls, spider_browser_open engine and stealth (0 auto-escalates to 3;
# 2 residential, 3 premium; its CAPTCHA solving is on by default)
#SPIDER_FINGERPRINT=1
#SPIDER_PROXY_ENABLED=0
#SPIDER_PROXY=residential
#SPIDER_COUNTRY_CODE=
#SPIDER_WAIT_IDLE_S=5
#SPIDER_BROWSER=auto
#SPIDER_BROWSER_STEALTH=0

# Read gate, hooks/read_gate.py (PreToolUse Read|Grep|Glob|Bash; read at each call, no reinstall; CONFIG.md
# "Read gate"). The first read of build output, dependency dirs, large data, media or binaries is refused
# with a cheaper alternative; the identical call repeated passes. Commented out = the default shown.
# 0 turns the gate off (READ_GATE=0 in the environment works too)
#READ_GATE=1
# Read limit / Grep head_limit at or under which a call passes; sizes in bytes above which text data
# (.csv .tsv .jsonl .json .xml .log .sql), lockfiles and images are gated (0 = never by size)
#READ_GATE_LIMIT=200
#READ_GATE_DATA_BYTES=262144
#READ_GATE_LOCK_BYTES=65536
#READ_GATE_IMAGE_BYTES=2097152
# refused calls remembered per session for the retry (oldest dropped)
#READ_GATE_MAX_KEYS=512
# agent types exempt per category, comma-separated; a value replaces the list
#READ_GATE_EXEMPT_BUILD=verifier,frontend-engineer,browser-operator
#READ_GATE_EXEMPT_DEPS=
#READ_GATE_EXEMPT_DATA=data-engineer,data-scientist,ml-engineer,dl-engineer,llm-engineer,mlx-engineer
#READ_GATE_EXEMPT_VISUAL=designer,motion-designer,image-director,cg-artist,doc-specialist
#READ_GATE_EXEMPT_BINARY=
# Optional: GitHub token raises libdocs' GitHub API limit (60/h unauthenticated). Any read-only token works.
# libdocs reads it from here; it is not exported to your shells (so agents' subprocesses don't hold a
# forge token) unless you add it to STACK_EXPORT below.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Hugging Face — Hub search and model/dataset details for the ML agents (mcp__huggingface) and the
# `hf` CLI. Read token is enough: https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens  (empty = anonymous Hub
# access; a token from `hf auth login` in ~/.cache/huggingface/token is used as a fallback).
# A revoked or wrong token is worse than none: the server then fails to connect (401).
HF_TOKEN=
# Weights & Biases — experiment tracking MCP (mcp__wandb) for ml-/dl-/llm-engineer. Optional;
# the server is registered only when this is set. https://wandb.ai/authorize
WANDB_API_KEY=

# Optional, for servers mounted on demand through mcp-broker (magg catalog; of this file, magg
# gets only these). Uncomment what you use; the JUPYTER_* and MLFLOW_* values are also
# exported into your shells (STACK_EXPORT). Absolute paths only: $VAR is not expanded here.
# jupyter — a JupyterLab/Jupyter Server you started yourself
#JUPYTER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888
#JUPYTER_TOKEN=
# mlflow — tracking server or store
#MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI=http://127.0.0.1:5000
# duckdb — MotherDuck cloud databases (md:); local files need nothing
#MOTHERDUCK_TOKEN=
# lean — a built Lean 4 + Mathlib Lake project for lean-lsp-mcp (proof-checker's own server and the
# magg catalog's; recommended). install.sh step 2 (STACK_INSTALL_LEAN) installs elan and, when this is
# unset, makes ~/lean/stack_mathlib (lake +stable new ... math; lake exe cache get; lake build): set it
# to that path or to a project of your own.
#LEAN_PROJECT_PATH=/Users/you/lean/project
# mongodb — official MongoDB server, read-only (a local or dev database; data-engineer's own server
# and the magg catalog's)
#MDB_MCP_CONNECTION_STRING=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mydb
# postgres — Postgres MCP Pro, restricted (read-only) mode (data-engineer's own server and magg's)
#DATABASE_URI=postgresql://user:password@127.0.0.1:5432/mydb
# qiskit-runtime — IBM Quantum Platform token (hardware jobs spend your quota). https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com
#QISKIT_IBM_TOKEN=
# godot — the Godot executable for godot-mcp
#GODOT_PATH=/Applications/Godot.app/Contents/MacOS/Godot
# sec-edgar — the User-Agent the SEC requires: "Name email"
#SEC_EDGAR_USER_AGENT="Your Name you@example.com"
# grafana — your Grafana and a Viewer service-account token (the server runs with --disable-write)
#GRAFANA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
#GRAFANA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=
# biomcp — optional NCBI key (higher PubMed rate limits). https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/account/
#NCBI_API_KEY=

# Keys your shell profile exports, for CLI tools (hf, wandb, gh, mlflow, jupyter): space-separated,
# "all" = every non-empty key. Default when unset:
#STACK_EXPORT="HF_TOKEN WANDB_API_KEY MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI JUPYTER_URL JUPYTER_TOKEN"

# equilibrium harness, container isolation (install.sh --with-eq-container writes these two lines after a verified install, unless
# you set them). A record of that install, read by doctor.sh: the harness takes its backend and images from its own flags.json
# (`eq_harness.py flags --container-images-env <state>/eq-container/image.env`), never from here.
# EQ_ISOLATION: container = the install verified the Apple `container` isolation (doctor.sh warns when it is set but the
# isolation is not installed or was skipped).
# EQ_IMAGE: the verified image that serves PF, as eq.invalid/<name>:<tag>@sha256:<64 hex>. Not a secret; not exported to your
# shells (STACK_EXPORT).
#EQ_ISOLATION=container
#EQ_IMAGE=
# equilibrium harness, WALL (the host-access broker and its one tunnel; install.sh --with-eq-container writes EQ_WALL=on and
# the three paths when it sets the WALL up, EQ_WALL=off when it does not; values you set are kept). The paths are the
# harness's own defaults; a moved one must be exported to the harness yourself (they are not in STACK_EXPORT).
#EQ_WALL=on
#EQ_TUNNEL_DIR=/Users/you/.cache/claude-agent-stack/eq-tunnel
#EQ_WALL_STATE_DIR=/Users/you/.local/state/claude-agent-stack/eq-wall
#EQ_WALL_DIR=/path/to/claude-agent-stack/lib/eq-wall
